# tela/src/tela.fab — the real tela library kernel (Branch B).
#
# Stage 1 U1 kernel contract: typed HTML/SVG view values + kernel-owned
# constructors. One FLAT module (G4-safe), stdlib-only — no
# norma/triga/faber-runtime dependency. The shape proven by the Stage 0
# U3 spike (spike/visus-b.fab) through all three lanes.
# (Import-free through U1; U3 adds ONE same-package sibling import —
# tela:validate — for the fail-closed glue, per the U3 write_scope.)
#
# Branch decision (frozen for Stage 1): Branch B — pure non-generic
# recursive View union + adjacent typed behavior plan. Branch A (generic
# View<Message>) remains the ideal end-state, blocked only by radix defect
# D1 (generic user-type construction does not instantiate type parameters;
# stage-0-branch-a-b-evidence.md §2/§4). If D1 lands, the Branch A re-spike
# is a DECISION POINT for a future stage — never a mid-stage switch.
#
# Policy locks applied (stage-0-protocol-policies.md):
#   (a) no raw-markup View variant — typed elements only, no RawHtml-style
#       escape in the ordinary path;
#   (b) Faber-Latin protocol spellings: name/value/children/tag_name
#       (tag_name avoids the D2 TS discriminant collision);
#   (d) Identity is an explicit typed field, never position-derived;
#       serialization into data-tela is the U3 serializer's job.
# Validation boundary: constructors stay open (no eager validation);
# fail-closed lexical/namespace checks land at the U2/U3 serializer edge.
#
# D2 re-check (2026-08-09, in-tree radix 0.80.0): TS emit +
# tsc --noEmit clean (exit 0) — tag_name emits with no discriminant
# collision against the TS `tag` discriminant. Workaround spelling kept.
# D3 re-check (2026-08-09, in-tree radix 0.80.0): Rust emit + scratch
# cargo check clean (Finished, exit 0) — new_identity routes the
# non-null identity through Identity ∪ null and emits Some(...)
# correctly. Direct non-null construction into the nullable field is
# STILL the D3 defect; the radix D3 delivery has NOT landed, so the
# new_identity workaround is KEPT for Stage 1 (recorded either way).
#
# Stage 1 U3 (serializer) notes — this module now carries the escape
# function, the deterministic HTML/CSS serializers, and the fail-closed
# boundary; it gains ONE same-package sibling import (tela:validate) for the
# namespace glue (stage-1-delivery.md U3 write_scope; U2 done_when (f)
# non-goal: the glue lives in the kernel module, keeping validate.fab G4-safe).
#
# Central escaping (policy (a)2): escape is the ONE escape path used by both
# text and attribute-value serialization. Escape sets: & < > for text;
# additionally " for attribute values (double-quote delimiters). No
# serializer path concatenates an unescaped value; the identity value
# flows through the same attribute-escape path (policy (d)).
#
# Fail-closed boundary (policy (a)2; U2 done_when (d)): before ANY emission
# the serializer runs the U2 lexical/namespace/void checks over the whole
# tree (validate_tree). html_view(View) → string ∪ null — null when any
# check fails, so invalid input NEVER emits markup (no partial output). The
# concrete mechanism (nullable result) is the implementing Hand's documented
# choice within the available Faber surface; exercised by exempla/serializer.fab.
#
# Property static posture (spec decision Q3): Property are carried in the
# tree as browser-lane DOM properties and are NOT serialized into static
# HTML in Stage 1. The spike's provisional data-prop: marker is SUPERSEDED.
# Recorded here for identity-hydration.md (U4).
#
# Emit-across-imports escalation (stage-1-delivery.md U3 done_when (g);
# coordination §2/§5): radix check resolves the tela:validate import, and the
# TS emit lane resolves it. The RUST emit lane re-analyzes the imported
# provider module WITHOUT the en reader pack (PARSE001 — provider-module
# locale propagation defect, reproduced with exempla/validation.fab on the
# in-tree radix 0.80.0) and fails CODEGEN001. Recorded + escalated to the
# radix lane. The contract remains covered by radix check + the import-free
# single-module proven lane (validate.fab, U2) + the TS lane; the exempla lane
# is not weakened.
#
# Verb collision (U3 done_when (h); G5/G6): `css` verified against the
# reserved-keyword registries — not reserved, kept verbatim (policy (b)).
# `html` COLLIDES with the Space.html enum-member top-level binding —
# the RECORDED G5 defect (stage-0-canary.md §5: "A `fn html()` collides with
# the Space.html member binding → SEM005.duplicate_definition. Naming
# constraint: prefix helpers (html_space)"). Applied workaround: the HTML
# renderer verb is html_view — the `html_` prefix per the G5 recorded
# constraint; the policy (b) English stem is preserved, NOT silently renamed.
# ESCALATED To mind: the G5 radix-lane fix restores the exact `html` verb
# (repro: SEM005.duplicate_definition on fn html(View) with
# Space.html). fix:g5 — removal = grep-replace after the radix fix.
#
# Stage 2 U1 (theme/token protocol) — this module gains the kernel-owned
# theme/token value types (Token/Theme), the core token baseline, and the
# fail-closed deterministic theme_css renderer. fix:<id> markers applied in
# this unit: fix:g5 — NONE (the theme verbs theme/token/theme_css were
# probed collision-free on in-tree radix 0.80.0; a free fn may share a class
# name — G5's collision is enum-member-specific); fix:codegen001 — the Rust
# emit-across-imports block persists (provider-module locale propagation),
# the Rust lane is attempted + recorded, the TS lane is the proven runtime
# lane. See the Stage 2 U1 section header for the pinned core baseline.
#
# Stage 2 U2 (product assembly + cascade) — this module gains the kernel-owned
# Bundle/Order carriers, bundle dedup by stable identity, the topo-ordering
# of library packages over the explicit orders map (stable-identity
# tie-break), and the assemble(...) → Style ∪ null product assembler with
# cascade layers: tela.reset (opt-in; v1 ships none) → tela.tokens → tela.
# components → library packages (topo) → tela.applicatio (last). fix:<id>
# markers: fix:g5 — NONE (assemble verb not reserved, probed); fix:codegen001
# — Rust emit-across-imports block persists (attempted + recorded).
#
# Stage 3 U1 (behavior carriers) — this module gains the pure behavior-plan
# carriers EventName/Effect/Update (closure-free, web:dom-free, G4-safe
# flat shape). fix:<id> markers: fix:g5 — NONE (all carrier verbs + variant
# names probed collision-free); fix:codegen001 — the Rust block persists.
# PARTIAL marks + the D1/EventBinding constraint are recorded in the Stage 3 U1
# section header.
# enum Space { html, svg } in one module; in-tree radix 0.80.0,
# 2026-08-09).

import from "tela:validate" private validate

# Space: namespace enum (html | svg). The open element model keeps the
# tag itself an ordinary string, so custom elements and future-standard
# names stay open.
enum Space {
    html,
    svg
}

# Attribute: serialized attribute — an (name, value) pair emitted into
# the tag.
class Attribute {
    string name
    string value
}

# Property: DOM property (value, checked, selection), modeled separately
# from serialized attributes because their semantics differ. Carried in
# the tree; not serialized into static HTML in Stage 1 (browser lane).
class Property {
    string name
    string value
}

# Identity: stable identity. Explicit typed field — never inferred from
# array position. Serialized in one documented hydration-ready form
# (data-tela) by the U3 serializer.
class Identity {
    string value
}

# View: the recursive tagged union at the center of the protocol.
# Closure-free (Branch B): behavior lives in the adjacent typed plan
# (Stage 3), not inside view values. No raw-markup variant (policy (a)).
union View {
    ElementNode {
        Identity ∪ null identity,
        Space space,
        string tag_name,
        list<Attribute> attributes,
        list<Property> properties,
        list<View> children
    },
    TextNode { string value },
    Fragment { list<View> children }
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kernel-owned constructors — ordinary functions over the same public view
# values. No compiler-known component kinds; helpers are not privileged
# syntax (policy: kernel owns constructors; G3 workaround).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn text_view(string value) → View {
    return variant TextNode { value = value } ∷ View
}

fn fragment_view(list<View> children) → View {
    return variant Fragment { children = children } ∷ View
}

# Typed stable identity via the nullable field. Routed through a helper
# that returns Identity ∪ null: direct non-null construction into a
# nullable union field is a known Rust-emission defect (D3 — Some() wrap
# missing; stage-0-branch-a-b-evidence.md §4). Workaround KEPT until the
# radix D3 delivery lands.
fn new_identity(string v) → Identity ∪ null {
    return Identity { value = v }
}

# Namespace values as plain helpers: enum-member access through an
# imported namespace does not resolve (gap G1), so the values are exposed
# as functions even inside the kernel module.
fn html_space() → Space {
    return Space.html
}

fn svg_space() → Space {
    return Space.svg
}

# Open-element shorthand: no identity, no attributes.
fn element_view(Space space, string tag_name, list<View> children) → View {
    return variant ElementNode {
        identity = null,
        space = space,
        tag_name = tag_name,
        attributes = [],
        properties = [],
        children = children
    } ∷ View
}

# Full open-element constructor: identity + serialized attributes + DOM
# properties. Cross-module variant construction does not resolve (gap G3),
# so consumers reach the full ElementNode through this constructor.
fn element_full(
    Identity ∪ null identity,
    Space space,
    string tag_name,
    list<Attribute> attributes,
    list<Property> properties,
    list<View> children
) → View {
    return variant ElementNode {
        identity = identity,
        space = space,
        tag_name = tag_name,
        attributes = attributes,
        properties = properties,
        children = children
    } ∷ View
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Central escaping (policy (a)2) — ONE escape path for text AND attribute
# values. in_attribute selects the attribute escape set: & < > for text,
# additionally " for attribute values (double-quote delimiters — HTML
# convention and Faber string syntax). The identity value flows through
# the same attribute-escape path (policy (d)). No serializer path
# concatenates an unescaped value.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn escape(string s, bool in_attribute) → string {
    var string accum ← ""
    var int i ← 0
    while i < s.length() {
        const string c ← s.slice(i, i + 1)
        # Sequential independent ifs (NOT an elif chain): the TS emitter
        # compiles elif chains whose bodies are single-statement assignments
        # into a returned conditional expression (undefined at runtime — a
        # NEW emitter observation, escalated to the radix lane). A single
        # character can match at most one branch, so the last match wins;
        # the sequential form is semantically identical and emits correctly
        # in both lanes (probe: escapa_elif vs escapa_seq, 2026-08-09).
        # The fragment starts as "" + c (NOT ← c): the Rust emitter moves a
        # String into a local via `let mut fragment: String = c;` while still
        # borrowing c below (E0382 — second NEW emitter observation,
        # escalated to the radix lane); "" + c emits a borrowing format!.
        var string fragment ← "" + c
        if c ≡ "&" then fragment ← "&amp;"
        if c ≡ "<" then fragment ← "&lt;"
        if c ≡ ">" then fragment ← "&gt;"
        # The quote comparison spells the double-quote via ascii-literal
        # interpolation ("§"('"')) rather than the \" escape: the TS emitter
        # double-escapes a `\"` string payload (emits `\\\"` → runtime
        # backslash+quote), so a bare-quote comparison written as "\"" never
        # matches at runtime (NEW emitter observation, escalated to the radix
        # lane; probe escape on in-tree radix 0.80.0, 2026-08-09).
        if in_attribute and c ≡ "§"('"') then fragment ← "&quot;"
        accum ← accum + fragment
        i ← i + 1
    }
    return accum
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Namespace glue (U3) — maps Space onto the U2 string surface and applies
# the fail-closed checks. validate.fab keeps its G4-safe string/bool surface.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn space_name(Space space) → string {
    match space {
        case Space.html {
            return "html"
        }
        case Space.svg {
            return "svg"
        }
    }
}

# Namespace emission: svg-namespace elements carry the xmlns declaration
# (spike baseline; required on the svg root for inline SVG, harmless on
# nested svg elements).
fn serialize_ns(Space space) → string {
    match space {
        case Space.html {
            return ""
        }
        case Space.svg {
            const string q ← "§"('"')
            return " xmlns=" + q + "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" + q
        }
    }
}

# Fail-closed pre-pass: the U2 lexical/namespace/void checks over the whole
# tree, run BEFORE any emission. Text is escaped, never validated (any
# string is safe once escaped — policy (a)2). Invalid input NEVER emits
# markup: html_view returns null (no output at all), never partial markup.
fn validate_tree(View v) → bool {
    match v {
        case TextNode const value {
            return true
        }
        case Fragment const children {
            for from children const child {
                if not validate_tree(child) then return false
            }
            return true
        }
        case ElementNode const identity, space, tag_name, attributes, properties, children {
            const string space_text ← space_name(space)
            if not validate.valid_space(space_text) then return false
            if not validate.valid_name_in_space(tag_name, space_text) then return false
            for from attributes const attr {
                if not validate.valid_attribute_name(attr.name) then return false
            }
            const bool has_children ← children.length() > 0
            if not validate.valid_void_structure(tag_name, has_children) then return false
            for from children const child {
                if not validate_tree(child) then return false
            }
            return true
        }
    }
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HTML serializer — deterministic, pure, over ordered lists. Author order of
# attributes and children is preserved; no unordered/hash iteration, no
# timestamps. Identical input → identical bytes (empirically proven by U6).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Attributes serialize as name="value" — double quotes (HTML convention
# and Faber string syntax). The value is escaped through the attribute set.
fn serialize_attributes(list<Attribute> attributes) → string {
    const string q ← "§"('"')
    var string accum ← ""
    for from attributes const attr {
        accum ← accum + " " + attr.name + "=" + q + escape(attr.value, true) + q
    }
    return accum
}

# Identity: non-null Identity emits data-tela="<escaped value>" — the ONE
# documented hydration-ready serialization form (policy (d)); elements
# without identity emit nothing.
fn serialize_identity(Identity ∪ null identity) → string {
    match identity {
        case null {
            return ""
        }
        default {
            const string q ← "§"('"')
            return " data-tela=" + q + escape(identity?.value coalesce "", true) + q
        }
    }
}

fn serialize_view(View v) → string {
    match v {
        case TextNode const value {
            return escape(value, false)
        }
        case Fragment const children {
            var string accum ← ""
            for from children const child {
                accum ← accum + serialize_view(child)
            }
            return accum
        }
        case ElementNode const identity, space, tag_name, attributes, properties, children {
            var string accum ← "<" + tag_name
            accum ← accum + serialize_ns(space)
            accum ← accum + serialize_attributes(attributes)
            accum ← accum + serialize_identity(identity)
            # Property are carried but NOT serialized into static HTML
            # (Stage 1 static posture; browser-lane DOM properties).
            var string inner ← ""
            for from children const child {
                inner ← inner + serialize_view(child)
            }
            if validate.valid_void_html(tag_name) then return accum + ">"
            return accum + ">" + inner + "</" + tag_name + ">"
        }
    }
}

# Public renderer verb (policy (b)). The G5 workaround spelling (html_view,
# html_ prefix per stage-0-canary.md §5) carries the locked English `html`
# stem; the exact `html` verb is blocked by the Space.html enum-member
# top-level binding and is ESCALATED to the radix lane (see module header).
# Fail-closed: null = rejected, no markup ever emitted. Deterministic bytes
# over author order.
fn html_view(View v) → string ∪ null {
    if not validate_tree(v) then return null
    return serialize_view(v)
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Initial CSS — smallest honest model (policy (c); spike shape). Deterministic
# emission preserving author order of rules and declarations.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

class Declaration {
    string name
    string value
}

class Rule {
    string selector
    list<Declaration> declarations
}

class Style {
    list<Rule> rules
}

fn css(Style style) → string {
    var string accum ← ""
    for from style.rules const rule {
        accum ← accum + rule.selector + " { "
        for from rule.declarations const decl {
            accum ← accum + decl.name + ": " + decl.value + "; "
        }
        accum ← accum + "}\n"
    }
    return accum
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stage 2 U1 — Theme / token protocol (campaign §5; policies (b)/(c)).
#
# CORE TOKEN BASELINE (v1 subset, pinned here): surface.canvas, surface.panel,
# text.primary, text.muted, border.default, accent.primary, state.positive,
# state.caution — the interoperable required set from the campaign §5
# families surface.* / text.* / border.* / accent.* / state.*. The v1 subset
# deliberately excludes the space/radius/type/motion families (small + honest;
# future subsets add tokens, never a closed enum — campaign §5).
#
# RENDERING CONVENTION (locked): a token name is a dotted path
# (chart.axis.muted); the CSS custom property is `--` + name with `.` → `-`
# (--chart-axis-muted — the U5-consumed spelling). theme_css emits the
# resolved tokens as one Rule { selector = ":root", … } — the selected root
# (campaign §5) — rendered through the existing css(Style) serializer.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED (U1 done_when (d)): a theme missing a required core token
# → null (no output); an invalid token name (fails the lexical predicate)
# → null. Deterministic bytes over author order (no unordered iteration).
#
# fix:<id> markers applied in this unit:
#   fix:g5 — none applied: the theme verbs (theme, token, theme_css) were
#            probed against the enum-member / reserved-keyword bindings on
#            in-tree radix 0.80.0 (a free fn may share a class name; G5's
#            collision is enum-member-specific) — no collision, no prefix
#            workaround needed. css stays the exact policy (b) verb.
#   fix:codegen001 — Rust emit-across-imports remains blocked (provider-module
#            locale propagation); the Rust lane is attempted + recorded; the
#            TS lane is the proven runtime lane. Never the gate, never
#            weakened.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Token: one named token — dotted path (chart.axis.muted) + resolved CSS
# value. U5-proven spelling (proof/benchmark/extension-lib declares the same
# shape).
class Token {
    string name
    string value
}

# Theme: a named theme — a token collection over the core baseline. Two
# materially different themes = two Theme values over the same component tree
# (campaign §5; variants are tokens, not new machinery).
class Theme {
    string name
    list<Token> tokens
}

# Kernel-owned constructors (G3 posture — ordinary functions over the same
# public values, like the Stage 1 kernel constructors).
fn token(string name, string value) → Token {
    return Token { name = name, value = value }
}

fn theme(string name, list<Token> tokens) → Theme {
    return Theme { name = name, tokens = tokens }
}

# The core token baseline — the required token set a theme must cover.
fn core_tokens() → list<string> {
    return [
        "surface.canvas", "surface.panel",
        "text.primary", "text.muted",
        "border.default",
        "accent.primary",
        "state.positive", "state.caution"
    ]
}

# Dotted path → CSS custom property: `--` + name with `.` → `-`.
# Deterministic single-character scan (sequential independent ifs — the
# TS-emitter-safe pattern from escape).
fn token_property_name(string name) → string {
    var string accum ← "--"
    var int i ← 0
    while i < name.length() {
        const string c ← name.slice(i, i + 1)
        if c ≡ "." then accum ← accum + "-"
        if c ≠ "." then accum ← accum + c
        i ← i + 1
    }
    return accum
}

# Token-name lexical shape: the dotted path must pass the U2 attribute-name
# predicate (letters/digits/-/_/. /:; rejects whitespace, =, quotes, < > /,
# and control characters). A token name is a dotted path, so the
# attribute-name charset covers it fail-closed.
fn token_name_valid(string name) → bool {
    return validate.valid_attribute_name(name)
}

# Required-token coverage: every core baseline token name must be present in
# the theme's token collection.
fn theme_has_core_tokens(Theme theme) → bool {
    const list<string> core ← core_tokens()
    var int i ← 0
    while i < core.length() {
        if not theme_has_token(theme, core[i]) then return false
        i ← i + 1
    }
    return true
}

fn theme_has_token(Theme theme, string name) → bool {
    for from theme.tokens const token {
        if token.name ≡ name then return true
    }
    return false
}

# Public theme renderer verb (policy (b)): emits the token layer as one
# :root Rule through the existing css(Style) serializer. Fail-closed:
# missing required core token, invalid token name, or duplicate token
# identity with different content → null (no output). Deterministic bytes
# over author order.
fn theme_css(Theme theme) → string ∪ null {
    const Style ∪ null style_result ← theme_style(theme)
    if style_result is null then return null
    return css(Style { rules = style_result!.rules })
}

# Token-layer builder shared by theme_css and the product assembler (U2):
# the :root Rule over the resolved tokens. Fail-closed: missing required
# core token → null; invalid token name → null; duplicate token identity —
# identical content dedups, different content rejects (policy (e); the
# stage-scope duplicate-token rule exercised here, the renderer surface).
# Deterministic bytes over author order.
fn theme_style(Theme theme) → Style ∪ null {
    if not theme_has_core_tokens(theme) then return null
    var list<Declaration> declarations ← vacua
    var int i ← 0
    while i < theme.tokens.length() {
        const Token token ← theme.tokens[i]
        if not token_name_valid(token.name) then return null
        var bool duplicate ← false
        var int j ← 0
        while j < i {
            const Token prior ← theme.tokens[j]
            if prior.name ≡ token.name {
                if prior.value ≠ token.value then return null
                duplicate ← true
            }
            j ← j + 1
        }
        if not duplicate {
            declarations.append(Declaration {
                name = token_property_name(token.name),
                value = token.value
            })
        }
        i ← i + 1
    }
    return Style {
        rules = [
            Rule { selector = ":root", declarations = declarations }
        ]
    }
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stage 2 U2 — Product assembly + cascade ordering (campaign §4/§7; policy (e)).
#
# assemble(...) → Style ∪ null — a PURE function over explicit inputs:
#   theme       — the selected theme (renders the tela.tokens :root layer)
#   reset       — optional reset bundle (tela.reset layer; v1 ships NO reset
#                 by default — the layer emits only when a bundle is given)
#   components  — component style bundles (tela.components layer, input order)
#   orders      — the explicit package-order map (identity + dependency list;
#                 stdlib-only, no import introspection)
#   bundles     — extension/library style bundles keyed by package identity
#                 (ordered by the orders topo map)
#   applicatio  — the optional application style bundle (last layer)
#
# CASCADE LAYER ORDER (emission order — later rules win, the deterministic
# cascade guarantee): tela.reset (opt-in) → tela.tokens (rendered :root
# token layer) → tela.components → extension/library packages in topo order
# → tela.applicatio (last). @layer at-rule syntax is NOT modeled in v1
# (policy (c) growth; recorded deferred).
#
# BUNDLE DEDUP (policy (e)): stable-identity dedup — identical duplicates
# (same rendered css) collapse to one; duplicate identity with different
# content rejects (fail-closed).
#
# LIBRARY ORDERING: dependency-graph topological order over the explicit
# orders map, then stable package identity as the deterministic
# content-independent tie-break (lexicographic via the list sorted
# intrinsic). Cycles, unknown dependencies, and bundle identities absent
# from the orders map reject (fail-closed).
#
# FAIL-CLOSED (U2 done_when (d)): dependency cycles reject; duplicate bundle
# identities with different content reject; duplicate token identities with
# different content reject (theme_style); identical duplicates dedup;
# invalid rule/declaration output (empty selector/property/value or CSS
# structure-breaking characters) rejects before emission.
#
# fix:<id> markers applied in this unit:
#   fix:g5 — NONE: the assemble verb verified against the reserved-keyword /
#            enum-member bindings on in-tree radix 0.80.0 (not reserved; the
#            probe in U1 established that a free fn may share a class name).
#   fix:codegen001 — Rust emit-across-imports remains blocked (provider-module
#            locale propagation); the Rust lane is attempted + recorded; the
#            TS lane is the proven runtime lane.
#   The nullable-result consumption (field-copy reconstructions) follows the
#   established kernel pattern (binding/argument `!` unwrap is rejected by
#   the parser — the same workaround family as the U1 theme_css wrapper).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A style bundle: stable identity + the Style it contributes. Stable
# identity = the locked package/bundle name (policy (e) — the deterministic
# tie-break basis).
class Bundle {
    string identity
    Style style
}

# One package-order entry: package identity + its dependency identities.
# The explicit package-order map — stdlib-only, no import introspection.
class Order {
    string identity
    list<string> depends
}

# Kernel-owned constructors (G3 posture).
fn bundle(string identity, Style style) → Bundle {
    return Bundle { identity = identity, style = style }
}

fn order(string identity, list<string> depends) → Order {
    return Order { identity = identity, depends = depends }
}

# Bundle dedup by stable identity (policy (e)): identical duplicates (same
# rendered css) collapse to one; duplicate identity with different content
# → null (fail-closed).
fn deduplicated_bundles(list<Bundle> bundles) → list<Bundle> ∪ null {
    var list<Bundle> unique ← vacua
    var int i ← 0
    while i < bundles.length() {
        const Bundle bundle ← bundles[i]
        var bool duplicate ← false
        var int j ← 0
        while j < i {
            const Bundle prior ← bundles[j]
            if prior.identity ≡ bundle.identity {
                if css(prior.style) ≠ css(bundle.style) then return null
                duplicate ← true
            }
            j ← j + 1
        }
        if not duplicate then unique.append(bundle)
        i ← i + 1
    }
    return unique
}

# Set membership over a string list.
fn in_list(list<string> collection, string name) → bool {
    for from collection const item {
        if item ≡ name then return true
    }
    return false
}

# A package is ready when its declared dependencies are all already placed.
# An identity absent from the orders map is never ready (its dependencies
# are unknowable) — the topo fails closed.
fn dependencies_met(list<Order> orders, string identity, list<string> placed) → bool {
    var int i ← 0
    while i < orders.length() {
        const Order order ← orders[i]
        if order.identity ≡ identity {
            var int j ← 0
            while j < order.depends.length() {
                if not in_list(placed, order.depends[j]) then return false
                j ← j + 1
            }
            return true
        }
        i ← i + 1
    }
    return false
}

# Topological order of the package identities over the explicit orders map
# (policy (e)): dependencies precede dependents; among the ready packages the
# stable identity is the deterministic tie-break (lexicographic via the list
# sorted intrinsic — content-independent). A cycle (no ready package while
# identities remain) or an unresolvable dependency → null (fail-closed).
fn topological_order(list<Order> orders, list<string> identities) → list<string> ∪ null {
    var list<string> ordered ← vacua
    var list<string> remaining ← identities
    while remaining.length() > 0 {
        var list<string> ready ← vacua
        var int i ← 0
        while i < remaining.length() {
            if dependencies_met(orders, remaining[i], ordered) then ready.append(remaining[i])
            i ← i + 1
        }
        if ready.length() ≡ 0 then return null
        ready ← ready.sorted()
        const string chosen ← ready[0]
        ordered.append(chosen)
        var list<string> next ← vacua
        var int j ← 0
        while j < remaining.length() {
            if remaining[j] ≠ chosen then next.append(remaining[j])
            j ← j + 1
        }
        remaining ← next
    }
    return ordered
}

# CSS-structure-breaking characters in selector / property / value text make
# the emitted rule invalid — fail-closed before emission.
fn unclean_style_text(string text) → bool {
    var int i ← 0
    while i < text.length() {
        const string c ← text.slice(i, i + 1)
        if c ≡ "{" then return true
        if c ≡ "}" then return true
        if c ≡ ";" then return true
        if c ≡ "\n" then return true
        i ← i + 1
    }
    return false
}

# Fail-closed rule/declaration shape: non-empty selector / property / value
# and no CSS-structure-breaking characters.
fn style_valid(Style style) → bool {
    for from style.rules const rule {
        if rule.selector.length() ≡ 0 then return false
        if unclean_style_text(rule.selector) then return false
        for from rule.declarations const decl {
            if decl.name.length() ≡ 0 then return false
            if decl.value.length() ≡ 0 then return false
            if unclean_style_text(decl.name) then return false
            if unclean_style_text(decl.value) then return false
        }
    }
    return true
}

# Find the Style a package identity contributes (the library-layer bundle
# lookup). Unknown identity → null (the topo already failed closed).
fn bundle_for(string identity, list<Bundle> bundles) → Style ∪ null {
    for from bundles const bundle {
        if bundle.identity ≡ identity then return bundle.style
    }
    return null
}

# Public product-assembly verb (policy (b); campaign §7): one ordered Style
# grouped in cascade-layer order. Fail-closed (no output on any reject).
# Deterministic bytes over the explicit inputs.
fn assemble(
    Theme theme,
    Style ∪ null reset,
    list<Bundle> components,
    list<Order> orders,
    list<Bundle> bundles,
    Bundle ∪ null applicatio
) → Style ∪ null {
    # tela.tokens — the rendered :root token layer (fail-closed).
    const Style ∪ null tokens_result ← theme_style(theme)
    if tokens_result is null then return null
    const Style tokens ← Style { rules = tokens_result!.rules }

    # tela.reset — validity only (emission order handled below); v1 ships no
    # reset by default, so the layer emits only when a bundle is provided.
    if reset not is null {
        if not style_valid(Style { rules = reset!.rules }) then return null
    }

    # tela.components — dedup + fail-closed rule shape, input order.
    const list<Bundle> ∪ null unique_components ← deduplicated_bundles(components)
    if unique_components is null then return null
    const list<Bundle> empty_components ← vacua
    const list<Bundle> final_components ← unique_components coalesce empty_components
    var int ci ← 0
    while ci < final_components.length() {
        if not style_valid(final_components[ci].style) then return null
        ci ← ci + 1
    }

    # Library packages — dedup, rule shape, then topo order over the explicit
    # orders map (every bundle identity must appear in orders).
    const list<Bundle> ∪ null unique_bundles ← deduplicated_bundles(bundles)
    if unique_bundles is null then return null
    const list<Bundle> empty_bundles ← vacua
    const list<Bundle> final_bundles ← unique_bundles coalesce empty_bundles
    var int li ← 0
    while li < final_bundles.length() {
        if not style_valid(final_bundles[li].style) then return null
        li ← li + 1
    }
    var list<string> identities ← vacua
    var int m ← 0
    while m < final_bundles.length() {
        identities.append(final_bundles[m].identity)
        m ← m + 1
    }
    const list<string> ∪ null libraries_result ← topological_order(orders, identities)
    if libraries_result is null then return null
    const list<string> empty_libraries ← vacua
    const list<string> libraries ← libraries_result coalesce empty_libraries

    # tela.applicatio — the last layer; validity only.
    if applicatio not is null {
        if not style_valid(Style { rules = applicatio!.style.rules }) then return null
    }

    # Assemble the ordered Style — layer order IS emission order.
    var list<Rule> rules ← vacua
    if reset not is null {
        for from reset!.rules const rule {
            rules.append(rule)
        }
    }
    for from tokens.rules const rule {
        rules.append(rule)
    }
    var int idx ← 0
    while idx < final_components.length() {
        for from final_components[idx].style.rules const rule {
            rules.append(rule)
        }
        idx ← idx + 1
    }
    var int ord ← 0
    while ord < libraries.length() {
        const Style ∪ null style ← bundle_for(libraries[ord], final_bundles)
        if style is null then return null
        for from style!.rules const rule {
            rules.append(rule)
        }
        ord ← ord + 1
    }
    if applicatio not is null {
        for from applicatio!.style.rules const rule {
            rules.append(rule)
        }
    }
    return Style { rules = rules }
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stage 3 U1 — Behavior carriers (behavior-design §2/§3; policy (b)).
#
# The pure behavior-plan carriers for the Branch B adjacent plan: EventName
# (the event value), Effect (declarative host effects keyed by stable
# identity), Update (the update result `replace` carries). Closure-free
# and web:dom-free — carriers are plain values; the kernel explicitly does
# NOT own EventBinding/Message/Program (the radix D1 generic-construction defect
# + the concrete-message-type constraint: the app-typed plan is U3's
# surface, recorded here, never kernel-generic).
#
# The carrier shapes match behavior-design §1/§3.2: the segmented-control
# contract (§1) consumes event names; the update strategy (§3.2) consumes
# the three declarative effects — focus restoration (by the pre-replacement
# focused node's stable identity), focus movement (to a declared target
# identity), scroll anchoring (scroll-preservation intent) — executed by the
# host after replacement, declarative on the update result, never imperative
# post-update user-code calls.
#
# PARTIAL marks (CTO Q2 bar, compile-level): the carrier shapes + kernel
# construction are fully proven here (exempla + lanes). The BEHAVIORAL
# EXECUTION of the effects (the host executing them; mount/replace/dispose;
# hydration) is PARTIAL at U1 — deferred to U2 (browser mount) + U3 (the
# segmented-interactive proof). No f64 arithmetic is involved in the
# carriers, so no f64 oracle pins apply.
#
# fix:<id> markers applied in this unit:
#   fix:g5 — NONE: the carrier verbs (event_name, restore, direct, anchor,
#            update) + variant names (Restore/Direct/Anchor) probed
#            collision-free on in-tree radix 0.80.0 (a free fn may share a
#            class name; union variants do not bind top-level the way enum
#            members do).
#   fix:codegen001 — Rust emit-across-imports remains blocked (attempted +
#            recorded); the TS lane is the proven runtime lane.
#   fix:sem001 — consumer-side match over an imported union's variants does
#            not bind (SEM001.unknown_variant); the kernel owns the only
#            matcher (effect_identity below) — the G3-family imported-
#            union matching gap, recorded + escalated to the radix lane.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# EventName: the event value — a typed event-name carrier (policy (b) locked
# spelling). Closure-free: an event carries a name, not behavior.
class EventName {
    string name
}

# Effect: the declarative host effects from behavior-design §3.2 — exactly
# three variants, each keyed by a stable identity (the data-tela value the
# Stage 1 hydration seam binds to):
#   Restore — focus restoration: restore focus to the node that held it
#     before replacement (by its stable identity);
#   Direct   — focus movement: move focus to a declared target identity;
#   Anchor   — scroll anchoring: scroll-preservation intent for the region.
union Effect {
    Restore { string identity },
    Direct { string identity },
    Anchor { string identity }
}

# Update: the update result `replace` carries (behavior-design §3) — the
# next view plus the declarative effects the host executes after
# replacement.
class Update {
    View view
    list<Effect> effects
}

# Kernel-owned constructors (G3 posture — ordinary functions over the same
# public values, like the Stage 1/2 kernel constructors; probed
# collision-free).
fn event_name(string name) → EventName {
    return EventName { name = name }
}

fn restore(string identity) → Effect {
    return variant Restore { identity = identity } ∷ Effect
}

fn direct(string identity) → Effect {
    return variant Direct { identity = identity } ∷ Effect
}

fn anchor(string identity) → Effect {
    return variant Anchor { identity = identity } ∷ Effect
}

# Effect-key accessor: the stable identity an effect keys to. The host
# executes effects by reading this key (U2 effect execution; U3 the
# segmented-interactive proof) — and the kernel is the only place a
# consumer-imported union's variants can be matched (the G3-family
# imported-union variant pattern does not bind from a consumer; recorded).
# fix:sem001 — removal = grep-replace after the radix fix lands.
# Total over the three variants — never null.
fn effect_identity(Effect effects) → string {
    match effects {
        case Restore const identity {
            return identity
        }
        case Direct const identity {
            return identity
        }
        case Anchor const identity {
            return identity
        }
    }
}

fn update(View view, list<Effect> effects) → Update {
    return Update { view = view, effects = effects }
}
